<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orbetello</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orbetello is a town in southern Tuscany, built on a narrow spit in the middle of a lagoon between the mainland and Monte Argentario. From 1557 it was the capital of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a>, which ringed it with powerful fortifications, and it was joined to Tuscany in the Napoleonic period. Its lagoon is now a nature reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orbetello.jpg" alt="Orbetello" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lorena Torres Angelini from Santiago, Chile · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OrbetelloWindmill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Orbetello was an Etruscan settlement dating from the 7th century BCE, and the massive polygonal city walls that still exist were built when it was refounded in the 4th century BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeated Vulci and confiscated its territory in 280 BCE, the Roman reorganisation of the area centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony of Cosa, founded near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansedonia">Ansedonia</a> in 273 BCE. The Domitii Ahenobarbi had a substantial property at Orbetello, which the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> inherited through his wife Domitia Longina, and he built other sumptuous villas nearby for his courtiers.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the Aldobrandeschi family held it until the 14th century, when the city of Orvieto acquired it. After several struggles with the Orsini of Pitigliano and with Orvieto, it was captured by the Sienese Republic. When Siena fell and its territory passed to Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> retained Orbetello, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talamone">Talamone</a>, Monte Argentario and the island of Giannutri, under the name of the Reali Stati dei Presidii, until 1713. Many Spanish names remained among the inhabitants. During the Franco-Spanish War the French besieged the town, which led to the inconclusive naval Battle of Orbetello on 14 June; in July a Spanish army forced the French to lift the siege.</p><p>The district passed by treaty to the emperor in 1713, to the king of the Two Sicilies in 1736, to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> in 1801 and to the Grand Duchy of Tuscany in 1814. It joined the newly unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1860. In 1927–33 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-balbo">Italo Balbo</a>'s air cruises started from the lagoon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Orbetello is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grosseto">Grosseto</a> in Tuscany, about 35 kilometres south of Grosseto, on a lagoon that is home to an important nature reserve. Its sights include the ancient city walls and the Cathedral of Santa Maria Assunta, built over an Etruscan-Roman temple and restructured in 1375 in Tuscan-Gothic style, which holds notable 15th-century frescoes. The Spanish Forte delle Saline stands at Albinia, and the remains of the Roman city of Cosa lie at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansedonia">Ansedonia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orbetello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Philip II of Spain granted the conquered Republic of Siena to Cosimo I de' Medici in 1557, he kept the coastal fortresses of <b>Orbetello</b>, Porto Ercole, Talamone, Porto Santo Stefano and Ansedonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a></li></ul></section>
